gpu_split: EDEN_NX2_SPLIT_QUIET=1 keeps other threads out of the scheduler while the back submits

Port of Djihads80/Eglantine df92fc6c95 (rdenno, PR #639, on nx2 since 2026-11-05). GPL-3.0-or-later,
as the core. The same race is PR #593 (A-Man-With-A-Plan-dev), still open.

The source commit: a second thread could use the scheduler while the back was submitting, which
crashed in Scheduler::SubmitExecution from GpuSplit::BackLoop -> Scheduler::FlushDeferred in Mario
Kart World and other games.

- The back ran its idle hook after publishing everything as consumed, so Drain() could return and
  the front run work of its own meanwhile. The back now clears back_quiet before it executes
  entries and sets it after the hook; Drain() waits for it and asks for the hook at once.
- BufferCache::WriteMemory reaches the scheduler from CPU and service threads. It now does so only
  on the thread that owns the scheduler (GpuSplit::OwnsScheduler).

Changed from the source:
- All of it hangs on EDEN_NX2_SPLIT_QUIET=1. Unset, BackQuiet() and OwnsScheduler() answer yes and
  Drain() and WriteMemory do what they did.
- 0019's drain report and 0083's inline page writes are in the same lines. The owner check also
  covers 0083's InlineMemoryImplementation, which records and submits like DownloadMemory.
- The back still runs its hook on every idle pass, as this tree did, not only when it was busy. So a
  RequestIdleHook made while it is quiet is not dropped.

With the switch on, the front is not the owner either while the back runs. MapInOrder's work runs on
the front after a Drain, so a WriteMemory from there marks the page CPU-modified without saving
GPU-written bytes beside it. The source does the same. Drain() also no longer lets the back batch
its deferred flush, so it may cost some fps. Neither is measured.

Core patch. Nothing here has run on a console.

